Hacking on moxie-cores
======================

What you need
-------------

- Verilator 5, for every simulation.
- The moxie-elf toolchain: binutils, GCC and newlib.
  `scripts/build-toolchain.sh PREFIX` builds it from release tarballs
  in about ten minutes, with tools/binutils-moxie-extensions.patch so
  that the assembler knows mox125's eret, wfi and cas.l.  Put
  PREFIX/bin on your PATH.
- SBCL and ocicl (https://github.com/ocicl/ocicl), for the mox125 test
  bench and the microcode generators.  After installing ocicl, run
  `ocicl setup > ~/.sbclrc`, and `ocicl install` in bench/mox125 and
  bench/icache to fetch their Lisp libraries.
- wrapilator (https://github.com/atgreen/wrapilator), built from git:
  its last release predates Verilator 5.
- FuseSoC, for the core files (`pip install fusesoc`), and Yosys,
  nextpnr, icestorm and trellis for the open-source FPGA builds.
- Xilinx ISE 14.7 for the Nexys 3, and Quartus II 13.0sp1, the last
  release that knows the Cyclone II, for the DE2.

.github/workflows/ci.yml sets all but the FPGA tools up on Fedora, and
is the most reliable recipe.


Running the tests
-----------------

`scripts/run-tests.sh` runs what CI runs: the mox125 and moxibit test
suites, the instruction set simulator's own check, and builds of the
other simulators.  The suites can be run on their own:

    make -C bench/moxibit check      # moxibit against the ISS
    make -C bench/moxibit lockstep   # ... every instruction, every register
    make -C bench/mox125 check       # mox125, in Common Lisp
    make -C tools/mox125-iss check   # the ISS against the bench programs

`scripts/fusesoc-check.sh` runs every FuseSoC lint and simulation
target.

The test programs are assembly, in bench/mox125/asm and
bench/moxibit/asm.  Each stores its result at 0x0c0ffee0 and ends by
writing to 0xdead; the result it should get is the comment on its
first line.


Changing a core
---------------

tools/mox125-iss and the cores have to agree on what every instruction
does: mox125's RTL is the reference for the ISS, and moxibit is checked
against the ISS.  Change both together.

mox125's microcode table is cores/mox125/microcode.org; `make` in
cores/mox125 regenerates microcode.bin.  moxibit's microcode is
cores/moxibit/microcode.org; `make` in cores/moxibit regenerates
moxibit_ucode.v and moxibit_ucode.vh.  Both generated files are
checked in, so nothing else needs SBCL.

When a moxibit test fails, `make lockstep` in bench/moxibit shows the
first instruction where the core and the ISS disagree, and the
registers either side of it.


Programming the boards
----------------------

Marin on the Nexys 3: `make program` in soc/marin/boards/nexys3 loads
the bitstream with Digilent's djtgcfg.

Marin on the DE2: `make` in soc/marin/boards/de2 builds marin.sof.
With Altera's jtagd running as root, `make program` there, or

    quartus_pgm -z -m JTAG -o "p;marin.sof"

loads it.  The on-chip boot loader shows FEED on the seven-segment
display and prints a message on the serial port at 9600 8N1.

Muskoka on the Nexys 3: see soc/muskoka/boards/nexys3/README.md.
Muskoka on the DE2: `make` and `make program` in
soc/muskoka/boards/de2; its boot loader talks at 115200 8N1.

Bala: the FuseSoC icebreaker and ulx3s targets build bitstreams (see
soc/bala/README.md); load them with the board's usual tool, such as
iceprog or openFPGALoader.  Its serial port runs at 115200 8N1.


Reporting bugs
--------------

Report bugs as GitHub issues: https://github.com/atgreen/moxie-cores/issues
